1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,560
Okay, Annette, Leroy, we are very excited to hear more about your program and how CLO

2
00:00:11,560 --> 00:00:12,560
has been used.

3
00:00:12,560 --> 00:00:14,700
So I'm handing over to you.

4
00:00:14,700 --> 00:00:16,879
You can start whenever you are ready.

5
00:00:16,879 --> 00:00:19,519
Okay, thank you, Elaine.

6
00:00:19,519 --> 00:00:21,160
Yes, we are ready.

7
00:00:21,160 --> 00:00:28,820
Okay, so hello, everyone, and today Leroy and I will talk to you about education, our

8
00:00:28,820 --> 00:00:34,000
education in virtual fashion design at our school, the Willem de Kooning Academy in Rotterdam,

9
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:35,719
the Netherlands.

10
00:00:35,719 --> 00:00:41,000
And first, let me briefly explain how our institute is designed.

11
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:46,100
So we have students that enter our school in their major.

12
00:00:46,100 --> 00:00:51,519
And there are many major, many design majors to choose from, like this could be graphic

13
00:00:51,519 --> 00:00:56,260
design, this could be advertising, and in our case, this is fashion design.

14
00:00:56,260 --> 00:00:58,820
And to educate our students in skills.

15
00:00:58,820 --> 00:01:04,980
We have our stations, or you could call it also workplaces, such as the print station,

16
00:01:04,980 --> 00:01:10,019
the metal workstation, and the fabric station, or the textile workplace, as you can see here

17
00:01:10,019 --> 00:01:11,420
on the screen.

18
00:01:11,420 --> 00:01:15,799
So all our workplaces and all our stations can be used by all our students.

19
00:01:15,799 --> 00:01:21,460
And that is how we encourage cross-disciplinary work.

20
00:01:21,460 --> 00:01:27,159
And of course, our fashion students, they will be found mostly in the fabric station.

21
00:01:27,159 --> 00:01:28,159
But when we are teaching virtual fashion design, they will be found mostly in the fabric station.

22
00:01:28,159 --> 00:01:28,260
But when we are teaching virtual fashion design, they will be found mostly in the fabric station.

23
00:01:28,820 --> 00:01:29,820
But when we are teaching virtual fashion design, they will be found mostly in the fabric station.

24
00:01:29,820 --> 00:01:33,920
So we need, in fact, the expertise from many stations.

25
00:01:33,920 --> 00:01:39,020
From the fabric station, we need tutors that specialize in patterns and in virtual pattern

26
00:01:39,020 --> 00:01:40,300
making.

27
00:01:40,300 --> 00:01:44,980
From our image and sound station, we need tutors that are skilled in making animations

28
00:01:44,980 --> 00:01:50,420
or games, and who can teach programs like Blender and 3D code.

29
00:01:50,420 --> 00:01:55,500
And in our interaction station, there are people specialized in VR and AR presentations

30
00:01:55,500 --> 00:01:58,160
who teach students how to work in Gravity Sketch.

31
00:01:58,159 --> 00:02:02,099
Or for example, how to print in 3D.

32
00:02:02,099 --> 00:02:05,019
So that is, I think, what makes our school quite unique.

33
00:02:05,019 --> 00:02:10,719
It enables students, after being trained in the necessary basic low 3D skills that are

34
00:02:10,719 --> 00:02:16,500
very much related to fashion and to patterns and fabrics, to explore with these acquired

35
00:02:16,500 --> 00:02:22,159
skills a whole new world of different kinds of mostly interdisciplinary projects in their

36
00:02:22,159 --> 00:02:24,319
own field of interest.

37
00:02:24,319 --> 00:02:26,639
And we have students that explore virtual materials.

38
00:02:26,639 --> 00:02:27,639
And we have students that explore virtual materials.

39
00:02:27,640 --> 00:02:32,320
And we have students that focus on virtual image making, on fashion in games, or on digital

40
00:02:32,320 --> 00:02:33,500
film.

41
00:02:33,500 --> 00:02:39,580
And we are not just educating for the fashion industry today, but much more for the creative

42
00:02:39,580 --> 00:02:41,500
industry of tomorrow.

43
00:02:41,500 --> 00:02:42,500
Leroy.

44
00:02:42,500 --> 00:02:43,500
Yes, hello.

45
00:02:43,500 --> 00:02:50,420
My name is Leroy, and I'm going to tell a little bit about one of the research projects

46
00:02:50,420 --> 00:02:54,840
that we are conducting around virtual fashion.

47
00:02:54,840 --> 00:02:57,580
Because to keep up with the rapid changing of changes in fashion, we need to focus on

48
00:02:57,640 --> 00:02:58,680
what we are doing in the digital world.

49
00:02:58,680 --> 00:02:59,680
We need to look at the digital world.

50
00:02:59,680 --> 00:03:00,680
We need to look at the digital world as a whole.

51
00:03:00,680 --> 00:03:01,680
But in the context of our creative industry, we as an academy are always looking for new

52
00:03:01,680 --> 00:03:06,720
perspectives and cutting-edge technologies to become a better version of ourselves.

53
00:03:06,720 --> 00:03:11,900
In this light, we have since last year been working on the Erasmus Plus research project

54
00:03:11,900 --> 00:03:19,520
Me, You, Us in collaboration with design school in Kolding in Denmark and University of Ljubljana

55
00:03:19,520 --> 00:03:20,920
in Slovenia.

56
00:03:20,920 --> 00:03:25,680
In this research project, we are searching for new methodologies to educate fashion design,

57
00:03:25,680 --> 00:03:27,500
use in digital tools and virtual reality.

58
00:03:27,500 --> 00:03:32,500
Here in Clo3D is one of our main programs that we use.

59
00:03:32,500 --> 00:03:38,500
However, virtual fashion design is not something new at Willem de Kooning Academy.

60
00:03:38,500 --> 00:03:47,500
We started our virtual journey with Clo3D already back in 2018 when we were introduced to the program at PI Apparel in Berlin.

61
00:03:47,500 --> 00:03:54,500
At that time, we saw that the potential of Clo3D, especially as something practical,

62
00:03:54,500 --> 00:03:58,500
let's say another way of expressing physical design.

63
00:03:58,500 --> 00:04:03,500
However, in recent years, we have been learning together with our students

64
00:04:03,500 --> 00:04:12,500
and have concluded that there's so much more potential in virtual design other than being the extension of physical fashion design.

65
00:04:12,500 --> 00:04:19,500
We came to understand that the virtual design world also needs a virtual design ontology.

66
00:04:19,500 --> 00:04:24,500
In other words, that is a virtual way of understanding the virtual world itself

67
00:04:24,500 --> 00:04:27,500
as well as the physical world around us.

68
00:04:27,500 --> 00:04:33,500
Perhaps it is in our Dutch nature, but it seems very logical to us

69
00:04:33,500 --> 00:04:39,500
that a tool like Clo3D or any other kind of virtual design that matter

70
00:04:39,500 --> 00:04:42,500
could also be used as a critical design tool.

71
00:04:42,500 --> 00:04:47,500
This critical perspective stands also at the core of the ME-US research project.

72
00:04:47,500 --> 00:04:52,500
The research is built up on the three pillars that we call our CIT goals,

73
00:04:52,500 --> 00:04:54,500
which stand for

74
00:04:54,500 --> 00:04:57,500
sustainability, inclusivity and diversity.

75
00:04:57,500 --> 00:05:02,500
Together with these other schools, we are exploring what these urgent themes mean today

76
00:05:02,500 --> 00:05:07,500
and what these themes will mean in the future of virtual fashion design.

77
00:05:07,500 --> 00:05:11,500
As we are still very early research data, as we would like,

78
00:05:11,500 --> 00:05:17,500
but we would love to take you along in our research later on.

79
00:05:17,500 --> 00:05:22,500
Okay, yeah, I'll take it from here again because, yes, today

80
00:05:22,500 --> 00:05:25,500
we really want to share the work of our students,

81
00:05:25,500 --> 00:05:30,500
just then instead of just telling how we do things and how we run things.

82
00:05:30,500 --> 00:05:34,500
So while we are talking, we will show student work.

83
00:05:34,500 --> 00:05:39,500
And for us, creativity and artistic development of our students takes center stage,

84
00:05:39,500 --> 00:05:45,500
and therefore we do not just focus on the technical advances of the program Clo3D,

85
00:05:45,500 --> 00:05:48,500
but rather on its creative potential.

86
00:05:48,500 --> 00:05:51,500
And to be clear, we, the Willem de Kooning Academy,

87
00:05:51,500 --> 00:05:53,500
is not a fashion institute.

88
00:05:53,500 --> 00:05:58,500
So we do not feel obliged to educate ready to go fashion professionals

89
00:05:58,500 --> 00:06:02,500
that will all inevitably work in the current fashion industry.

90
00:06:02,500 --> 00:06:07,500
Of course, they might choose to do so, and we support them in that too, of course.

91
00:06:07,500 --> 00:06:09,500
But that is not our full intention.

92
00:06:09,500 --> 00:06:13,500
We are an art school that trains fully rounded creatives

93
00:06:13,500 --> 00:06:17,500
that might later in life become a fashion designer.

94
00:06:17,500 --> 00:06:20,500
So for us, a good student knows how to use their creativity

95
00:06:20,500 --> 00:06:22,500
to set action in motion,

96
00:06:22,500 --> 00:06:25,500
and to use design as a means to tell a story,

97
00:06:25,500 --> 00:06:27,500
to be critical to the world around them,

98
00:06:27,500 --> 00:06:31,500
and to be a part of the solution rather than the problem.

99
00:06:31,500 --> 00:06:36,500
Therefore, we train them to, well, firstly to work sufficiently in Clo3D

100
00:06:36,500 --> 00:06:38,500
to visualize their designs,

101
00:06:38,500 --> 00:06:42,500
but also at the same time we challenge them to hack the program

102
00:06:42,500 --> 00:06:46,500
and use Clo3D to challenge society's notions of fashion.

103
00:06:46,500 --> 00:06:49,500
And this is very exciting to us.

104
00:06:49,500 --> 00:06:52,500
It might be our rebellious Dutch nature,

105
00:06:52,500 --> 00:06:55,500
but we like to challenge the parameters of systems

106
00:06:55,500 --> 00:06:58,500
and to disturb existing hierarchies

107
00:06:58,500 --> 00:07:01,500
and to see what lays beyond the known.

108
00:07:01,500 --> 00:07:04,500
To us, it is a system embedded in fashion

109
00:07:04,500 --> 00:07:07,500
to challenge the current state of fashion.

110
00:07:07,500 --> 00:07:11,500
And in order to do so, we have embedded Clo3D

111
00:07:11,500 --> 00:07:16,500
in three of the four years of our fashion bachelor course

112
00:07:16,500 --> 00:07:18,500
with a possible fourth year,

113
00:07:18,500 --> 00:07:23,500
when a student chooses to use Clo3D for their graduation project.

114
00:07:23,500 --> 00:07:28,500
And just like any other fashion course that uses Clo3D in its curriculum,

115
00:07:28,500 --> 00:07:31,500
we acknowledge that there is still a great gap

116
00:07:31,500 --> 00:07:36,500
between physical making and the use of virtual prototyping.

117
00:07:36,500 --> 00:07:41,500
So Leroy is one of the professors who uses Clo3D in his classes

118
00:07:41,500 --> 00:07:45,500
and who encounters this discrepancy in his classes.

119
00:07:45,500 --> 00:07:47,500
Leroy, could you tell a little bit more

120
00:07:47,500 --> 00:07:50,500
how you try to bring the physical making

121
00:07:50,500 --> 00:07:53,500
and the virtual making together in your classes?

122
00:07:53,500 --> 00:07:55,500
Well, thank you, Annette.

123
00:07:55,500 --> 00:07:58,500
I will try to do so.

124
00:07:58,500 --> 00:08:02,500
It is indeed true that one of the biggest problems

125
00:08:02,500 --> 00:08:06,500
that we face in our education

126
00:08:06,500 --> 00:08:12,500
is this shift towards virtual and digital design

127
00:08:12,500 --> 00:08:14,500
in our education curriculum

128
00:08:14,500 --> 00:08:17,500
was the gap between physical and virtual making.

129
00:08:17,500 --> 00:08:19,500
From an outside perspective,

130
00:08:19,500 --> 00:08:21,500
this gap might seem insignificant,

131
00:08:21,500 --> 00:08:23,500
but it poses a real problem

132
00:08:23,500 --> 00:08:26,500
and a headbreaker for us as a department.

133
00:08:26,500 --> 00:08:27,500
To be fully honest,

134
00:08:27,500 --> 00:08:29,500
we are still searching for answers

135
00:08:29,500 --> 00:08:31,500
and I cannot provide a full answer today.

136
00:08:31,500 --> 00:08:34,500
That's also why we do this research project.

137
00:08:34,500 --> 00:08:37,500
But I can say this.

138
00:08:37,500 --> 00:08:38,500
From our experience,

139
00:08:38,500 --> 00:08:40,500
we have learned that the divide

140
00:08:40,500 --> 00:08:42,500
between physical and virtual making

141
00:08:42,500 --> 00:08:44,500
is not just a question of technology

142
00:08:44,500 --> 00:08:46,500
that can be solved by one of the many features

143
00:08:46,500 --> 00:08:48,500
of the Clo3D program.

144
00:08:48,500 --> 00:08:49,500
For example,

145
00:08:49,500 --> 00:08:52,500
students can easily create patterns in Clo3D

146
00:08:52,500 --> 00:08:55,500
and print them in order to stitch their digital sites

147
00:08:55,500 --> 00:08:56,500
in physical samples.

148
00:08:56,500 --> 00:08:59,500
This is very easy for them.

149
00:08:59,500 --> 00:09:02,500
This is not the problem,

150
00:09:02,500 --> 00:09:04,500
but the actual problem lays much deeper

151
00:09:04,500 --> 00:09:06,500
in the inner world of the students' creativity

152
00:09:06,500 --> 00:09:08,500
and design intuition.

153
00:09:08,500 --> 00:09:09,500
Over the years,

154
00:09:09,500 --> 00:09:12,500
we have noticed that Clo3D was often seen

155
00:09:12,500 --> 00:09:15,500
as an additional skill next to their physical design practice.

156
00:09:15,500 --> 00:09:20,500
We still see that there is still a lot of skepticism

157
00:09:20,500 --> 00:09:25,500
from a more traditional corner of our fashion industry.

158
00:09:25,500 --> 00:09:28,500
Virtual making was just another way

159
00:09:28,500 --> 00:09:31,500
of expressing physical fashion design.

160
00:09:31,500 --> 00:09:35,500
We also still see these attitudes towards virtual design

161
00:09:35,500 --> 00:09:37,500
in many fashion institutes.

162
00:09:37,500 --> 00:09:40,500
Virtual design is just in many cases

163
00:09:40,500 --> 00:09:43,500
a kind of add-on in education,

164
00:09:43,500 --> 00:09:45,500
kind of a nice way

165
00:09:45,500 --> 00:09:47,500
to present a physical dress

166
00:09:47,500 --> 00:09:50,500
that you could make for real.

167
00:09:50,500 --> 00:09:52,500
On the other side,

168
00:09:52,500 --> 00:09:53,500
as an academy,

169
00:09:53,500 --> 00:09:55,500
we see that the next generation

170
00:09:55,500 --> 00:09:57,500
who grew up with the technology

171
00:09:57,500 --> 00:09:59,500
is able to use tech already

172
00:09:59,500 --> 00:10:01,500
as their second nature.

173
00:10:01,500 --> 00:10:02,500
In the Netherlands today,

174
00:10:02,500 --> 00:10:04,500
a six-year-old child often knows

175
00:10:04,500 --> 00:10:06,500
how to use a tablet

176
00:10:06,500 --> 00:10:09,500
before they know how to write.

177
00:10:09,500 --> 00:10:11,500
The future is clearly already here.

178
00:10:11,500 --> 00:10:13,500
At the Wim de Kooning Academy,

179
00:10:13,500 --> 00:10:15,500
we feel the need to respond to the need

180
00:10:15,500 --> 00:10:18,500
to respond to this rapidly changing world.

181
00:10:18,500 --> 00:10:20,500
Therefore, we have chosen to include

182
00:10:20,500 --> 00:10:22,500
virtual and digital communication

183
00:10:22,500 --> 00:10:27,500
in the basics of our fashion major education,

184
00:10:27,500 --> 00:10:29,500
right next to physical skills.

185
00:10:29,500 --> 00:10:31,500
By simultaneously teaching students

186
00:10:31,500 --> 00:10:33,500
practical skills like pattern drawing

187
00:10:33,500 --> 00:10:36,500
in physical and virtual reality,

188
00:10:36,500 --> 00:10:39,500
we hope to annul this divide altogether

189
00:10:39,500 --> 00:10:43,500
by creating a parallel design intuition.

190
00:10:43,500 --> 00:10:44,500
We hope,

191
00:10:44,500 --> 00:10:47,500
by continuously switching back and forth

192
00:10:47,500 --> 00:10:49,500
between physical and virtual reality,

193
00:10:49,500 --> 00:10:52,500
that students no longer feel this division,

194
00:10:52,500 --> 00:10:53,500
but rather see them

195
00:10:53,500 --> 00:10:56,500
as an extension of one another.

196
00:10:56,500 --> 00:10:58,500
One and the same thing.

197
00:10:58,500 --> 00:11:00,500
Well, that's what we hope.

198
00:11:00,500 --> 00:11:01,500
Annette will now tell you

199
00:11:01,500 --> 00:11:02,500
how we do this

200
00:11:02,500 --> 00:11:04,500
in the first year design assignment

201
00:11:04,500 --> 00:11:07,500
called Bending for Functionality.

202
00:11:07,500 --> 00:11:08,500
And whilst I was having this talk,

203
00:11:08,500 --> 00:11:09,500
you can see the work

204
00:11:09,500 --> 00:11:10,500
of one of our students

205
00:11:10,500 --> 00:11:13,500
called Besa Demirci.

206
00:11:14,500 --> 00:11:17,500
Thanks.

207
00:11:17,500 --> 00:11:18,500
I think so.

208
00:11:18,500 --> 00:11:20,500
Now back to you.

209
00:11:20,500 --> 00:11:21,500
From the very first day

210
00:11:21,500 --> 00:11:22,500
that our students entered

211
00:11:22,500 --> 00:11:24,500
the fashion department,

212
00:11:24,500 --> 00:11:25,500
we introduced them to the idea

213
00:11:25,500 --> 00:11:27,500
of virtual technology.

214
00:11:27,500 --> 00:11:28,500
And for a matter of fact,

215
00:11:28,500 --> 00:11:29,500
we have heard from students

216
00:11:29,500 --> 00:11:31,500
that they really choose for our school

217
00:11:31,500 --> 00:11:33,500
for its virtual and digital approach

218
00:11:33,500 --> 00:11:35,500
to fashion.

219
00:11:35,500 --> 00:11:36,500
As we said,

220
00:11:36,500 --> 00:11:38,500
our major fashion design education

221
00:11:38,500 --> 00:11:40,500
is roughly three years,

222
00:11:40,500 --> 00:11:43,500
and we introduce virtual fashion mythologies

223
00:11:43,500 --> 00:11:45,500
in block 1.3.

224
00:11:45,500 --> 00:11:46,500
So that is in the first year,

225
00:11:46,500 --> 00:11:49,500
just before the summer holiday.

226
00:11:49,500 --> 00:11:50,500
And of course,

227
00:11:50,500 --> 00:11:52,500
we do introduce digital technical skills

228
00:11:52,500 --> 00:11:53,500
like pattern drawing

229
00:11:53,500 --> 00:11:54,500
and the basics of clothes

230
00:11:54,500 --> 00:11:57,500
with the earlier on in the year.

231
00:11:57,500 --> 00:11:58,500
But to guarantee

232
00:11:58,500 --> 00:12:00,500
an inclusive learning environment,

233
00:12:00,500 --> 00:12:02,500
we must presume that students

234
00:12:02,500 --> 00:12:04,500
still need to learn all these skills,

235
00:12:04,500 --> 00:12:06,500
even though they might already

236
00:12:06,500 --> 00:12:07,500
be very experienced

237
00:12:07,500 --> 00:12:09,500
in virtual pattern drawing.

238
00:12:09,500 --> 00:12:11,500
And as we mentioned before,

239
00:12:11,500 --> 00:12:12,500
these practical skills are taught

240
00:12:12,500 --> 00:12:13,500
at our school

241
00:12:13,500 --> 00:12:15,500
by our station instructors.

242
00:12:15,500 --> 00:12:17,500
And so for the first year students,

243
00:12:17,500 --> 00:12:19,500
these are Paulina Martina,

244
00:12:19,500 --> 00:12:20,500
who has a background

245
00:12:20,500 --> 00:12:22,500
in speculative VR design

246
00:12:22,500 --> 00:12:24,500
and is an expert on AI.

247
00:12:24,500 --> 00:12:25,500
And Geert van der Meer,

248
00:12:25,500 --> 00:12:27,500
who is a professional pattern drawing

249
00:12:27,500 --> 00:12:28,500
with a great knowledge

250
00:12:28,500 --> 00:12:31,500
of digitizing patterns.

251
00:12:31,500 --> 00:12:33,500
So when our students know

252
00:12:33,500 --> 00:12:35,500
the basics of both the physical

253
00:12:35,500 --> 00:12:37,500
and the virtual fashion design,

254
00:12:37,500 --> 00:12:40,500
our fashion design tutors come in.

255
00:12:40,500 --> 00:12:42,500
For the banding functionality project,

256
00:12:42,500 --> 00:12:45,500
these are Selina Parr and Anouk van Klaveren.

257
00:12:45,500 --> 00:12:46,500
And together,

258
00:12:46,500 --> 00:12:48,500
they will introduce our students

259
00:12:48,500 --> 00:12:50,500
to this parallel methodology

260
00:12:50,500 --> 00:12:52,500
of designing fashions.

261
00:12:52,500 --> 00:12:55,500
So the focus of our first year project

262
00:12:55,500 --> 00:12:57,500
is that students learn

263
00:12:57,500 --> 00:12:59,500
to critically question

264
00:12:59,500 --> 00:13:01,500
the function of everyday garments.

265
00:13:01,500 --> 00:13:02,500
And this research,

266
00:13:02,500 --> 00:13:04,500
they start in the physical realm

267
00:13:04,500 --> 00:13:06,500
with Selina Parr

268
00:13:06,500 --> 00:13:09,500
and in Cloth3D with Anouk van Klaveren.

269
00:13:09,500 --> 00:13:11,500
In the physical starting class,

270
00:13:11,500 --> 00:13:14,500
they start with dissecting an iconic garment

271
00:13:14,500 --> 00:13:16,500
and questioning its functionality

272
00:13:16,500 --> 00:13:18,500
through physical methods.

273
00:13:18,500 --> 00:13:20,500
This could be through draping with the garment,

274
00:13:20,500 --> 00:13:23,500
cutting it up, making collages,

275
00:13:23,500 --> 00:13:24,500
sketching on paper,

276
00:13:24,500 --> 00:13:27,500
just physical ways of expressing a design.

277
00:13:27,500 --> 00:13:30,500
And then also quite traditionally,

278
00:13:30,500 --> 00:13:32,500
they sample this garment in Calico

279
00:13:32,500 --> 00:13:34,500
for which they will have to make

280
00:13:34,500 --> 00:13:37,500
the pattern by hand on paper.

281
00:13:37,500 --> 00:13:40,500
And only then they will digitize

282
00:13:40,500 --> 00:13:42,500
the paper and patterns into Cloth3D

283
00:13:42,500 --> 00:13:45,500
and make a 3D render of their design.

284
00:13:45,500 --> 00:13:47,500
Because there is also a physical Calico sample

285
00:13:47,500 --> 00:13:48,500
at that time,

286
00:13:48,500 --> 00:13:50,500
the tutors can really compare

287
00:13:50,500 --> 00:13:52,500
this virtual 3D model

288
00:13:52,500 --> 00:13:54,500
to its physical Calico counterparts.

289
00:13:54,500 --> 00:13:56,500
And this provides the tutors

290
00:13:56,500 --> 00:13:58,500
and the instructors with an actual reference

291
00:13:58,500 --> 00:14:01,500
to measure the 3D garment too.

292
00:14:01,500 --> 00:14:03,500
Okay, while this is all happening

293
00:14:03,500 --> 00:14:04,500
at the same time,

294
00:14:04,500 --> 00:14:06,500
as Selina's classes go on.

295
00:14:06,500 --> 00:14:07,500
In Anouk's classes,

296
00:14:07,500 --> 00:14:09,500
students start with a virtual garment

297
00:14:09,500 --> 00:14:12,500
that they morph and dissect and transform

298
00:14:12,500 --> 00:14:15,500
with the tools in Cloth3D.

299
00:14:15,500 --> 00:14:18,500
We try to cultivate the same curiosity

300
00:14:18,500 --> 00:14:20,500
and spontaneity in virtual space

301
00:14:20,500 --> 00:14:21,500
as our students also have

302
00:14:21,500 --> 00:14:23,500
in the physical assignments.

303
00:14:23,500 --> 00:14:25,500
Although students are playing around

304
00:14:25,500 --> 00:14:28,500
with patterns in the 2D box of Cloth3D,

305
00:14:28,500 --> 00:14:30,500
physical makeability is not a concern to us

306
00:14:30,500 --> 00:14:33,500
in this virtual exploratory phase.

307
00:14:33,500 --> 00:14:35,500
Students first need to visualize

308
00:14:35,500 --> 00:14:37,500
their ideas in virtual space.

309
00:14:37,500 --> 00:14:38,500
And then in the second half

310
00:14:38,500 --> 00:14:39,500
of the semester,

311
00:14:39,500 --> 00:14:41,500
they will adapt these virtual designs

312
00:14:41,500 --> 00:14:43,500
into makeable patterns

313
00:14:43,500 --> 00:14:46,500
that can be printed and executed in fabric.

314
00:14:46,500 --> 00:14:48,500
So with one tutor,

315
00:14:48,500 --> 00:14:51,500
the students go from physical to virtual.

316
00:14:51,500 --> 00:14:53,500
And with the other tutor,

317
00:14:53,500 --> 00:14:56,500
they go from virtual to physical.

318
00:14:56,500 --> 00:14:57,500
And these processes,

319
00:14:57,500 --> 00:14:58,500
they run parallel.

320
00:14:58,500 --> 00:15:00,500
So at any given point in this project,

321
00:15:00,500 --> 00:15:02,500
they are doing each segment,

322
00:15:02,500 --> 00:15:04,500
both in physical space

323
00:15:04,500 --> 00:15:07,500
and in virtual space at the same time.

324
00:15:08,500 --> 00:15:12,500
And then I will continue for year two.

325
00:15:12,500 --> 00:15:14,500
The second year project

326
00:15:14,500 --> 00:15:16,500
called Future of Fashion.

327
00:15:16,500 --> 00:15:18,500
And I have a few videos

328
00:15:18,500 --> 00:15:20,500
that I will show in the meantime

329
00:15:20,500 --> 00:15:23,500
because they are working on animations.

330
00:15:23,500 --> 00:15:25,500
The second year project

331
00:15:25,500 --> 00:15:26,500
called Future of Fashion

332
00:15:26,500 --> 00:15:28,500
primarily focuses on the power

333
00:15:28,500 --> 00:15:30,500
of speculation in fashion design

334
00:15:30,500 --> 00:15:31,500
and virtual fashion design

335
00:15:31,500 --> 00:15:33,500
as a device for storytelling.

336
00:15:33,500 --> 00:15:36,500
The brief to our students is quite simple.

337
00:15:36,500 --> 00:15:37,500
Come up with a future scenario

338
00:15:37,500 --> 00:15:38,500
of the world

339
00:15:38,500 --> 00:15:40,500
and design a virtual collection

340
00:15:40,500 --> 00:15:43,500
that represents the speculative scenario.

341
00:15:43,500 --> 00:15:45,500
The final project

342
00:15:45,500 --> 00:15:46,500
of Future of Fashion project

343
00:15:46,500 --> 00:15:48,500
is an animation showcasing

344
00:15:48,500 --> 00:15:49,500
this digital collection

345
00:15:49,500 --> 00:15:52,500
in a self-created virtual environment.

346
00:15:52,500 --> 00:15:56,500
Here we see the work of Zala Meshan.

347
00:15:56,500 --> 00:15:57,500
Guiding this project

348
00:15:57,500 --> 00:15:59,500
are Sreena Par.

349
00:15:59,500 --> 00:16:02,500
And I go now to the next video.

350
00:16:02,500 --> 00:16:05,500
Yes.

351
00:16:05,500 --> 00:16:06,500
Guiding this project

352
00:16:06,500 --> 00:16:07,500
are Sreena Par

353
00:16:07,500 --> 00:16:09,500
on the collection design front.

354
00:16:09,500 --> 00:16:11,500
And Esther van Brake

355
00:16:11,500 --> 00:16:12,500
who is actually an alumni

356
00:16:12,500 --> 00:16:13,500
from Willem de Kooning

357
00:16:13,500 --> 00:16:16,500
who already in 2017

358
00:16:16,500 --> 00:16:17,500
graduated herself

359
00:16:17,500 --> 00:16:19,500
with a virtual fashion project.

360
00:16:19,500 --> 00:16:21,500
She now teaches concept development

361
00:16:21,500 --> 00:16:24,500
and Natalia van Coren

362
00:16:24,500 --> 00:16:26,500
who will focus on circular economies

363
00:16:26,500 --> 00:16:29,500
and sustainability in this project.

364
00:16:29,500 --> 00:16:32,500
What we hope to do in this project

365
00:16:32,500 --> 00:16:34,500
is not only focus on the problems

366
00:16:34,500 --> 00:16:35,500
of the world today,

367
00:16:35,500 --> 00:16:37,500
which is quite easily,

368
00:16:37,500 --> 00:16:40,500
you only have to kind of look outside to see it,

369
00:16:40,500 --> 00:16:41,500
but we ask the students

370
00:16:41,500 --> 00:16:45,500
to speculate about the problems of tomorrow

371
00:16:45,500 --> 00:16:48,500
from the perspective of today.

372
00:16:48,500 --> 00:16:49,500
For some students,

373
00:16:49,500 --> 00:16:51,500
this means that they try to come up

374
00:16:51,500 --> 00:16:53,500
with a solution-driven concept

375
00:16:53,500 --> 00:16:55,500
to battle the impact of problems

376
00:16:55,500 --> 00:16:57,500
such as rising sea levels

377
00:16:57,500 --> 00:16:59,500
or reducing biodiversity.

378
00:16:59,500 --> 00:17:00,500
However, other students

379
00:17:00,500 --> 00:17:02,500
use this Future of Fashion project

380
00:17:02,500 --> 00:17:04,500
as a platform for social commentary,

381
00:17:04,500 --> 00:17:07,500
through grotesque exaggeration and humor,

382
00:17:07,500 --> 00:17:10,500
designing a parody of contemporary culture.

383
00:17:10,500 --> 00:17:12,500
For example, here we see

384
00:17:12,500 --> 00:17:14,500
the project of Philip Malmgren

385
00:17:14,500 --> 00:17:16,500
who really kind of explores the parameters

386
00:17:16,500 --> 00:17:19,500
and the limitations of the Closed2D program

387
00:17:19,500 --> 00:17:23,500
through making kind of a parody out of it.

388
00:17:23,500 --> 00:17:26,500
On a more practical skill-based level,

389
00:17:26,500 --> 00:17:28,500
we want to teach our students

390
00:17:28,500 --> 00:17:29,500
how to seamlessly switch

391
00:17:29,500 --> 00:17:31,500
between different software and methods

392
00:17:31,500 --> 00:17:33,500
of making digital images.

393
00:17:33,500 --> 00:17:35,500
Again, we want to cultivate

394
00:17:35,500 --> 00:17:37,500
a digital intuition in our students

395
00:17:37,500 --> 00:17:39,500
and provide different ways

396
00:17:39,500 --> 00:17:44,500
of making digital images contribute to this.

397
00:17:44,500 --> 00:17:46,500
For example, students design

398
00:17:46,500 --> 00:17:49,500
and make their clothes in Closed2D.

399
00:17:49,500 --> 00:17:51,500
They create environments

400
00:17:51,500 --> 00:17:53,500
and those virtual worlds in Blender,

401
00:17:53,500 --> 00:17:55,500
but also they sculpt accessories

402
00:17:55,500 --> 00:17:58,500
in graffiti sketch or Rhino.

403
00:17:58,500 --> 00:17:59,500
And in the end,

404
00:17:59,500 --> 00:18:01,500
use a multitude of different engines

405
00:18:01,500 --> 00:18:03,500
to make their animation videos

406
00:18:03,500 --> 00:18:04,500
come to life.

407
00:18:04,500 --> 00:18:06,500
This, of course,

408
00:18:06,500 --> 00:18:08,500
is all highly technical

409
00:18:08,500 --> 00:18:10,500
and goes somewhat beyond

410
00:18:10,500 --> 00:18:11,500
the technical capabilities

411
00:18:11,500 --> 00:18:13,500
of our fashion tutors.

412
00:18:13,500 --> 00:18:16,500
Also, my technical capabilities.

413
00:18:16,500 --> 00:18:18,500
But at Willem de Koon Academy,

414
00:18:18,500 --> 00:18:20,500
we are very lucky to have stations

415
00:18:20,500 --> 00:18:23,500
where there is plenty of expert knowledge

416
00:18:23,500 --> 00:18:25,500
on Closed2D programming

417
00:18:25,500 --> 00:18:27,500
and virtual design methods.

418
00:18:27,500 --> 00:18:29,500
For this project,

419
00:18:29,500 --> 00:18:30,500
we work closely with

420
00:18:30,500 --> 00:18:32,500
the Image and Sound Station.

421
00:18:32,500 --> 00:18:34,500
This is represented by Peter Schropp,

422
00:18:34,500 --> 00:18:36,500
who specializes in animations and games,

423
00:18:36,500 --> 00:18:38,500
and with the Interaction Station

424
00:18:38,500 --> 00:18:41,500
represented by Brigitte Lichtenegger,

425
00:18:41,500 --> 00:18:45,500
Joris Butter and Mike Pelletier,

426
00:18:45,500 --> 00:18:47,500
all experts in AR, VR,

427
00:18:47,500 --> 00:18:49,500
and interaction design.

428
00:18:54,500 --> 00:18:56,500
Yeah, so last part

429
00:18:56,500 --> 00:18:58,500
is in fact the year three part.

430
00:19:02,500 --> 00:19:04,500
The last real assignment

431
00:19:04,500 --> 00:19:06,500
in our fashion design major

432
00:19:06,500 --> 00:19:08,500
we call the major assignment,

433
00:19:08,500 --> 00:19:10,500
partially due to its enormous size,

434
00:19:10,500 --> 00:19:12,500
but also because all the knowledge

435
00:19:12,500 --> 00:19:14,500
that the students have obtained

436
00:19:14,500 --> 00:19:16,500
so far accumulates in this project.

437
00:19:16,500 --> 00:19:17,500
And it is also the moment

438
00:19:17,500 --> 00:19:19,500
that students really have to start

439
00:19:19,500 --> 00:19:21,500
thinking about professionalization

440
00:19:21,500 --> 00:19:23,500
in preparation for their internships

441
00:19:23,500 --> 00:19:26,500
the second half of the third year.

442
00:19:26,500 --> 00:19:28,500
So we believe that with professionalization

443
00:19:28,500 --> 00:19:31,500
comes a great degree of specialization.

444
00:19:31,500 --> 00:19:33,500
And we acknowledge that our program

445
00:19:33,500 --> 00:19:35,500
can be very demanding for our students.

446
00:19:35,500 --> 00:19:36,500
And therefore,

447
00:19:36,500 --> 00:19:37,500
we ask them to choose

448
00:19:37,500 --> 00:19:39,500
either a physical track

449
00:19:39,500 --> 00:19:40,500
in which they will focus

450
00:19:40,500 --> 00:19:42,500
on making a physical collection

451
00:19:42,500 --> 00:19:44,500
or a virtual track

452
00:19:44,500 --> 00:19:45,500
in which they will work

453
00:19:45,500 --> 00:19:48,500
on an immersive virtual fashion experience.

454
00:19:48,500 --> 00:19:50,500
And by this time,

455
00:19:50,500 --> 00:19:52,500
students already know much better

456
00:19:52,500 --> 00:19:54,500
what their strengths are.

457
00:19:54,500 --> 00:19:56,500
And we see that they quite naturally

458
00:19:56,500 --> 00:19:58,500
choose for their own preference.

459
00:19:58,500 --> 00:20:00,500
The choice to split the course

460
00:20:00,500 --> 00:20:02,500
into physical and virtual is because

461
00:20:02,500 --> 00:20:03,500
we believe that in this year

462
00:20:03,500 --> 00:20:05,500
of professionalization,

463
00:20:05,500 --> 00:20:06,500
it should be up to the student

464
00:20:06,500 --> 00:20:09,500
to choose what they want to specialize in.

465
00:20:09,500 --> 00:20:10,500
And by this time,

466
00:20:10,500 --> 00:20:12,500
the station classes have become

467
00:20:12,500 --> 00:20:13,500
so incredibly expert

468
00:20:13,500 --> 00:20:14,500
that we acknowledge

469
00:20:14,500 --> 00:20:16,500
that they are only useful

470
00:20:16,500 --> 00:20:18,500
for those who really want to continue

471
00:20:18,500 --> 00:20:20,500
in virtual fashion design.

472
00:20:20,500 --> 00:20:22,500
However, we see an increasing demand

473
00:20:22,500 --> 00:20:24,500
and interest in our students

474
00:20:24,500 --> 00:20:25,500
with our students

475
00:20:25,500 --> 00:20:28,500
to follow these classes.

476
00:20:28,500 --> 00:20:30,500
And although we make it

477
00:20:30,500 --> 00:20:31,500
a division between physical

478
00:20:31,500 --> 00:20:33,500
and virtual projects,

479
00:20:33,500 --> 00:20:34,500
these two tracks,

480
00:20:34,500 --> 00:20:36,500
they really start off together

481
00:20:36,500 --> 00:20:39,500
in an extensive conceptual phase.

482
00:20:39,500 --> 00:20:40,500
As we said before,

483
00:20:40,500 --> 00:20:42,500
we believe that the design process

484
00:20:42,500 --> 00:20:44,500
of physical and virtual fashion

485
00:20:44,500 --> 00:20:46,500
is the same.

486
00:20:46,500 --> 00:20:48,500
They only diverge

487
00:20:48,500 --> 00:20:50,500
in a technical execution.

488
00:20:50,500 --> 00:20:51,500
The fashion tutors

489
00:20:51,500 --> 00:20:52,500
that are assigned to this project

490
00:20:52,500 --> 00:20:54,500
are Leroy van Halen,

491
00:20:54,500 --> 00:20:57,500
who's sitting next to me here,

492
00:20:57,500 --> 00:20:59,500
and who, next to teaching,

493
00:20:59,500 --> 00:21:00,500
also works as a teacher

494
00:21:00,500 --> 00:21:01,500
as a fashion designer

495
00:21:01,500 --> 00:21:03,500
for Parisian luxury brands.

496
00:21:03,500 --> 00:21:05,500
And there is Anouk van Klaveren,

497
00:21:05,500 --> 00:21:07,500
who also has an experimental

498
00:21:07,500 --> 00:21:08,500
fashion-based practice

499
00:21:08,500 --> 00:21:10,500
that focuses on exhibitions

500
00:21:10,500 --> 00:21:14,500
and fashion in the public context.

501
00:21:14,500 --> 00:21:17,500
Yes, thank you, Annette.

502
00:21:17,500 --> 00:21:20,500
Yes, you saw a few examples

503
00:21:20,500 --> 00:21:22,500
of projects that we did.

504
00:21:22,500 --> 00:21:23,500
And as I said,

505
00:21:23,500 --> 00:21:25,500
we really kind of wanted to make,

506
00:21:25,500 --> 00:21:26,500
well, in this assignment,

507
00:21:26,500 --> 00:21:29,500
we do very kind of expert,

508
00:21:29,500 --> 00:21:32,500
immersive virtual fashion experiences.

509
00:21:32,500 --> 00:21:35,500
And we want to kind of show you

510
00:21:35,500 --> 00:21:37,500
all one particular project.

511
00:21:37,500 --> 00:21:41,500
This project is from Nino van Litt.

512
00:21:41,500 --> 00:21:43,500
And Nino van Litt is a student of us

513
00:21:43,500 --> 00:21:46,500
that really manages to very well

514
00:21:46,500 --> 00:21:48,500
come up with a very unique perspective

515
00:21:48,500 --> 00:21:50,500
of what he wants

516
00:21:50,500 --> 00:21:54,500
and uses all different kinds of platforms

517
00:21:54,500 --> 00:21:56,500
to make, in the end,

518
00:21:56,500 --> 00:21:58,500
he made a game.

519
00:21:58,500 --> 00:22:00,500
But he made the clothes,

520
00:22:00,500 --> 00:22:02,500
which are very speculative clothes.

521
00:22:02,500 --> 00:22:03,500
He made the garments,

522
00:22:03,500 --> 00:22:06,500
for example, in Cloth3D.

523
00:22:06,500 --> 00:22:09,500
And then he, well, first of all,

524
00:22:09,500 --> 00:22:12,500
he started off already kind of digital

525
00:22:12,500 --> 00:22:14,500
by using a lot of digital materials.

526
00:22:14,500 --> 00:22:15,500
His collection is, in the end,

527
00:22:15,500 --> 00:22:17,500
about stock image

528
00:22:17,500 --> 00:22:19,500
and digital material culture

529
00:22:19,500 --> 00:22:21,500
that he then kind of imported

530
00:22:21,500 --> 00:22:24,500
into designs of made in Cloth3D.

531
00:22:24,500 --> 00:22:26,500
And then he used lots of different

532
00:22:26,500 --> 00:22:27,500
engines and programs,

533
00:22:27,500 --> 00:22:29,500
but predominantly Unity

534
00:22:29,500 --> 00:22:33,500
to make a playable game out of it.

535
00:22:33,500 --> 00:22:34,500
We are going to show you

536
00:22:34,500 --> 00:22:36,500
a little video about,

537
00:22:36,500 --> 00:22:38,500
well, of his game.

538
00:22:38,500 --> 00:22:39,500
And in the corner,

539
00:22:39,500 --> 00:22:42,500
you see the student himself.

540
00:22:42,500 --> 00:22:43,500
Hey, what's up, guys?

541
00:22:43,500 --> 00:22:44,500
It's Scarce here.

542
00:22:44,500 --> 00:22:46,500
And today, I hope you're doing well.

543
00:22:46,500 --> 00:22:48,500
And it's time for a reaction

544
00:22:48,500 --> 00:22:50,500
to an interesting post,

545
00:22:50,500 --> 00:22:52,500
a thought piece that I saw

546
00:22:52,500 --> 00:22:56,500
on the internet on our subreddit.

547
00:22:56,500 --> 00:22:57,500
Huge holidays.

548
00:22:57,500 --> 00:22:59,500
Look it up.

549
00:22:59,500 --> 00:23:03,500
Chris, where are you going?

550
00:23:03,500 --> 00:23:04,500
Oh, my God.

551
00:23:04,500 --> 00:23:09,500
What is going on?

552
00:23:09,500 --> 00:23:11,500
Going to take a few hours to get 20K?

553
00:23:11,500 --> 00:23:19,500
Oh, you bet your ass it will, yeah.

554
00:23:19,500 --> 00:23:23,500
Adequate for 20K.

555
00:23:23,500 --> 00:23:24,500
That's a bad guy.

556
00:23:24,500 --> 00:23:25,500
When he comes into my shop,

557
00:23:25,500 --> 00:23:26,500
he causes trouble.

558
00:23:26,500 --> 00:23:32,500
Rolling 80 of these

559
00:23:32,500 --> 00:23:33,500
is going to be the biggest

560
00:23:33,500 --> 00:23:35,500
fucking pain in the ass.

561
00:23:35,500 --> 00:23:36,500
Oh, my God.

562
00:23:47,500 --> 00:23:48,500
Yeah, baby.

563
00:23:48,500 --> 00:23:49,500
That's what I've been waiting for.

564
00:23:49,500 --> 00:23:50,500
That's what it's all about.

565
00:23:54,500 --> 00:23:55,500
So here you can really see

566
00:23:55,500 --> 00:23:58,500
